* Your team commits to 40 story points but completes only 25. How would you investigate and improve future Sprint planning?
* A Product Owner adds a critical feature halfway through the Sprint. How would you handle the request?
* A production incident requires several developers to stop their planned Sprint work. How would you manage the Sprint Goal?
* Developers and QA engineers disagree about when a story is considered complete. How would you resolve the issue?
* Your team repeatedly carries unfinished Java development stories into the next Sprint. What would you change?
* A senior developer assigns story points based on coding effort alone, ignoring testing and integration. How would you improve estimation?
* A developer refuses to participate in Daily Scrum meetings because they consider them unnecessary. How would you address this?
* The Product Owner presents user stories with unclear acceptance criteria just before Sprint Planning. What would you do?
* A critical microservice dependency is delayed by another team. How would you protect your Sprint Goal?
* Your team has high velocity but increasing production defects. How would you address the quality problem?
* A team member estimates every task significantly higher than the rest of the team. How would you facilitate estimation?
* A developer completes a story but has not written unit tests or updated documentation. Would you consider it done?
* Stakeholders request a fixed release date before the team has estimated the backlog. How would you respond?
* A team member is on leave after Sprint Planning, reducing available capacity significantly. How would you adjust the plan?
* During a retrospective, developers blame the Product Owner for all delivery failures. How would you facilitate a constructive discussion?
* Your team identifies recurring blockers in every retrospective but does not implement the agreed actions. How would you improve accountability?
* A new Java developer joins during an active Sprint and needs onboarding support. How would you manage their work?
* Two Scrum Teams are changing the same shared API and repeatedly breaking each other's integration tests. How would you resolve the coordination problem?
* Management compares individual developers using story points completed. How would you explain why this is misleading?
* Your team spends most of each Sprint fixing defects rather than delivering planned features. How would you address the underlying causes?
* A security vulnerability requires immediate work that was not included in the Sprint Backlog. How would you prioritize it?
* A Sprint Goal becomes irrelevant because business priorities change significantly. How should the team respond?
* A team consistently meets Sprint commitments but delivers features that do not satisfy users. How would you improve outcomes?
* A Product Owner is unavailable during the Sprint, and developers are blocked by business questions. What would you do?
* A manager insists that all stories must be completed within the Sprint even when requirements change. How would you handle this expectation?
* Your distributed team struggles to attend Daily Scrum at a mutually convenient time. How would you maintain effective coordination?
* A critical integration story is marked complete by development but fails in the shared test environment. How would you address the Definition of Done?
* The team wants to increase velocity by reducing code review and testing effort. How would you respond?
* A large database migration cannot be safely completed within one Sprint. How would you break down and plan the work?
* You join a Scrum Team with low morale, poor predictability, and frequent conflicts. What would your first 30 days look like?